ctor of security there  he'd gotten thousands of people out and  then he went back into the tower and was  never seen again and hearing that fact  just hearing that little bit made a  profound impression on me because I  immediately realized that this was  someone who after saving so many people  he could have left the World Trade  Center then and there and had been  acclaimed as a hero and had been alive  today and yet for some reason he went  back and gave his life trying to save  others as much there was tremendous  amount of heroism that day I don't need  to tell you that but this was an example  of someone virtually unique of  many stories I have heard who was under  no obligation to go back into those  towers it was not part of his job  description to plunge into that inferno  and yet he did this this this just  trigger two thoughts in my mind first of  all in my experience that the best  stories I've written have always begun  as something of a mystery a question  such as why would someone behave in a  certain way how did something happen  something that is very difficult to  understand and in this case I wanted to  know what kind of person was this and  why would he make the choice to risk his  own life and to face almost certain  death to save others  and secondly I recognize this in its  broad outlines as one of the most  elemental stories that has been told  throughout human history and that is the  story of an individual human being who  chooses to give up their own life for  others in other words in its very  essence it suggests that there are  causes in life that are more important  than our own individual survival and  that has been at the heart of many of  the great stories that have been told  throughout mankind now I'm an admirer of  the work of Tom Wolfe his excellent  nonfiction writer and has also become an  excellent fiction writer I teach a  course in writing at Columbia and Tom  Wolfe came one year to speak to my  students for which I was very grateful  he also he made the point in my class  and he has also written a famous essay  in Harper's in which he criticized many  fiction writers for always looking more  inward looking at the smaller stories  and failing to capture the sweep of  modern history and society in the way  it's a great novelist of the past had  danzo la Balzac Dickens trollop he said  that we had a generation of writers who  were not facing these kinds of stories  for whatever reason and he challenged  nonfiction writers in his essay in my  class to step into this bridge to find  the true stories in our world today that  capture the broad sweep of human  experience  and in doing so to convey in the  nonfiction format all of the truths and  the power that fiction writers has often  have often given us but I have to tell  you as a nonfiction writer the  opportunity to accept that challenge the  opportunities when you are dealing with  fact are very unusual but even when I  first heard this story I had a glimmer  of an idea that perhaps in this the  greatest tragedy of my lifetime and in  the life of Rick was corrala there was  possibly the opportunity to tell that  kind of story I began my reporting um by  getting into contact with Rick's widow  Susan I had gotten her name from someone  at Morgan Stanley Morgan Stanley oddly  enough did not really want me to pursue  this story they did not want anyone to  focus the tragedy and their role in it  particularly but they gave me the name  of students Widow and I called her in  New Jersey and this was a difficult call  to make as you can imagine someone had  just lost her husband I had learned that  they had only been married for three  years but Susan agreed that she would  talk to me if I came out on the train  now I have to say that I've often been  in a reporting situation where someone  weeps across as an investigative  reporter often writing about  white-collar crime I had to say in many  cases they've been weeping because they  were arrested or they got caught but I  consider myself relatively stoic and you  know I've been able to get through these  situations so I arrived Susan met me at  the Train and she was very lovely very  gracious she seemed very calm under the  circumstance as we went to the home that  she had shared with Rick she showed me  many pictures many things related to him  we sat down and started to talk and  predictably within five minutes Susan  was weeping the twist in the story was  that in seven minutes I was weeping as  well and we were basically just handing  the box of Kleenexes back and forth  several hours later I was on the train  back to New York I looked down in my  notebook and I saw I had written about  two sentences and I realized I'm never  gonna be able to make a story out of the  it had been a very emotional experience  but nonetheless a very valuable one  because I think we did become very close  in those first meetings and I think two  seasons immense credit she eventually  shared virtually everything about her  life and her amazing relationship with  Rick the book opens with the scene where  Susan having been twice divorced was  walking her dog and a very mysterious  stranger passed by he was running in his  bare feet and to this day Susan doesn't  know why she did it but she spoke out  and said why are you running in your  bare feet and it turned out it was Rick  and he was writing a play about his  experience in Africa where he had  observed the natives in Africa off and  running in their bare feet and he wanted  to see what it felt like and naturally  that was a pretty intriguing story and  it launched a really remarkably intense  wonderful late-in-life romance between  Rick and Susan some of you you know may  be roughly my age may have read the  famous Newsweek story which said that  single women over 40 the odds of meeting  a man are lower than being struck by  lightning let me tell you that Rick and  Susan prove that that isn't that isn't  true late in the reporting I ended up  turning this into a story for The New  Yorker that was really focused on the  relationship between Rick and Susan but  rather late am i reporting for this  story Susan also mentioned that Rick had  had this amazingly close best friend  named Dan Hill and I spoke to Dan on the  phone to find out some more information  about Rick and he made an impression on  me even on the phone he lived in st.  Augustine and I remember saying several  times to Dan I said Dan you've really  had a pretty remarkable life it was  quite modest and he didn't really seem  to agree with that and but he appeared  as a character in the story although not  as a major character in the story after  the story came out he was coming up to  New York to see Susan and we were still  talking so we agreed to have dinner in  Manhattan  and I thought probably I had told this  story as much as it needed to be told  but I met Dan for dinner and Dan told me  a few more stories and I don't think  I've ever told him this but at the end  of that meal I'd hadn't even brought a  notebook I didn't write anything down  but I the first thing the next morning I  called my editor at Simon &amp; Schuster and  I said this has got to be a book you  have got to let me turn this into a book  and very shortly after that I arranged  to come spend a weekend with Dan his  wife Patricia who is also here here  today to hear more of these amazing  stories of the life and Rickon Dan that  had preceded citizens having that met  her husband including many stories I  don't think Susan herself even knew so I  landed here in in Jacksonville dan met  me at the airport and he had insisted  that I spend the weekend with with him  and his wife at their home  I haven't actually told dan this but  from past experience as a reporter I've  learned that it's sometimes it's very  risky to agree to spend the weekend with  someone's home you never quite know what  you're getting into I had a good feeling  based on my early meetings with Dan but  you never know so we pulled up to his  house and we went inside and one of the  first things I noticed is he was showing  me around the house was his quite  extensive gun collection which I'll  admit made me slightly nervous but I  think as a reporter you you learn to  trust certain instincts and within 5 or  10 minutes I I felt like part of the  family  really and ended up having a wonderful  weekend with Dan one of the stories that  he told me which was completely  unexpected was how he had met Ric when  he was working an amazing capacity and  as an undercover operative in the Congo  in 1960 and Ric was fighting in Rhodesia  with the British police force there they  met after a rugby game became instant  close friends one of these almost  magical encounters in life that Ric  seemed to experience and Dan moved in  with Ric for a while  in the barracks in what was then  northern rhodesia one day one of the  natives came in to say that a lion was  taraf terrorizing a nearby village and  could Rick do anything to help so Rick  went to the hardware store whatever it  was the general store I got this  double-barreled shotgun hopped into the  back of the Land Rover with a guy and  off they'd want to find this blond and  you can read the whole story and all its  glory in the book but to make a long  story short the lion was there that some  down the line appeared Rick had this  plan where he was going to be in this  blind and they would fire as the as the  law and attacked a a goat that they had  tied up in the middle of a clearing  but his adrenaline was rushing and for  some reason before the lion had a chance  to attack the goat Rick just leaped out  into the clearing exposing himself to  the wine the wine shifted its attention  and made a massive spring towards Rick  he had two shots in the in the barrel  double-barreled gun and he fired and the  the lion was killed and fell at his feet  so he was a hero when he came back to  the village but