ne I called my editor in New York  and I said this has got to be the title  it was almost as though Rick himself he  did in fact name the book that would be  about him before he died now in this  material in the vivid recollections of  Susan the vivid recollections of down  the raw materials of the notebooks as  well as many others I interviewed every  surviving member of Rick's platoon in  Vietnam I interviewed people who had  worked with him he had an amazing life  in various turning points in world  history  I interviewed so many that people  remembered him who also knew Dan who  nooses and I realized at some point that  I finally I felt did have the raw  material to write a story that if not  entirely living up to it to Wolf's  charge would at least attempt to grapple  with some of those big themes  I had read last year in another book by  Jacques Barzun a book called  from dawn to decadence it's a social and  cultural history of the West from Middle  Ages to the president he had said that  the notion of the epoch no longer exists  in our culture because we live in such a  fragmented society that no one can agree  on any one cause it would be worth  giving one's life for the notion of the  epic the hero in classic literature was  someone who chooses duty over  self-interest and that Duty reflected  something some principle some value the  transcended divisions and in fact  unified people for the greater good I  was thinking about this just last week  when I went to a production in New York  of the Trojans which tells the very  familiar Greek story of the Trojan horse  coming into Troy Cassandra the the  character who warns of impending  disaster but no one will listen and then  the hero Aeneas who goes to Carthage and  falls in love with with Dido  but because the gods speak to him that  he has a greater distant he leaves the  woman he loves behind and sails to Italy  where he founds what becomes the Roman  Empire and I saw so many parallels not  obvious ones but clear ones to the story  of Rick dan and Susan one of the  fascinating parts of the book which I  won't repeat because you can read it but  is the way that van and Rick  investigated the security at the World  Trade Center before 1993 and saw the  weaknesses Dan when and consulted with  Rick again after the bombing in 1993 and  in a written report they warned of the  possibility of a plane crashing in to  those towers these were the warnings of  impending doom that no one would listen  to in New York and believe me I believe  the story of how the Port Authority  responded to security issues at the  World Trade Center still remains to be  told many questions have not been  answered but just like Cassandra these  were the warnings that went unheeded and  then at that pivotal moment with chaos  all around him and tried  looming Rick had to make that very  personal and in my view heroic decision  to leave behind the people he cared most  about the woman he loved and his best  friend just as Aeneas had to leave  behind Dido  and he went back into the tower in  pursuit of a cause I think we could  agree despite what bars on said was a  cause worth dying for  he saved 3,700 lives that day even  though he gave his own and I'm not one  also to necessarily believe in in  destiny and yet in seeing the facts of  this story and I hope you will ponder  this yourself I often was amazed at the  twists of fade the way that Rick somehow  escaped death in amazing circumstances  so that he was alive at that moment in  history were his particular brand of  courage and principled integrity was so  much needed I don't like to say what  this book means exactly because I think  like a novel that's something that that  readers need to to see for themselves  but it certainly caused me to reflect on  made many big issues I grew up in the in  the era of Vietnam War protests I don't  think I really ever understood the  Vietnam War from the point of view of  people who fought there until I got 