y grateful  he also he made the point in my class  and he has also written a famous essay  in Harper's in which he criticized many  fiction writers for always looking more  inward looking at the smaller stories  and failing to capture the sweep of  modern history and society in the way  it's a great novelist of the past had  danzo la Balzac Dickens trollop he said  that we had a generation of writers who  were not facing these kinds of stories  for whatever reason and he challenged  nonfiction writers in his essay in my  class to step into this bridge to find  the true stories in our world today that  capture the broad sweep of human  experience  and in doing so to convey in the  nonfiction format all of the truths and  the power that fiction writers has often  have often given us but I have to tell  you as a nonfiction writer the  opportunity to accept that challenge the  opportunities when you are dealing with  fact are very unusual but even when I  first heard this story I had a glimmer  of an idea that perhaps in this the  greatest tragedy of my lifetime and in  the life of Rick was corrala there was  possibly the opportunity to tell that  kind of story I began my reporting um by  getting into contact with Rick's widow  Susan I had gotten her name from someone  at Morgan Stanley Morgan Stanley oddly  enough did not really want me to pursue  this story they did not want anyone to  focus the tragedy and their role in it  particularly but they gave me the name  of students Widow and I called her in  New Jersey and this was a difficult call  to make as you can imagine someone had  just lost her husband I had learned that  they had only been married for three  years but Susan agreed that she would  talk to me if I came out on the train  now I have to say that I've often been  in a reporting situation where someone  weeps across as an investigative  reporter often writing about  white-collar crime I had to say in many  cases they've been weeping because they  were arrested or they got caught but I  consider myself relatively stoic and you  know I've been able to get through these  situations so I arrived Susan met me at  the Train and she was very lovely very  gracious she seemed very calm under the  circumstance as we went to the home that  she had shared with Rick she showed me  many pictures many things related to him  we sat down and started to talk and  predictably within five minutes Susan  was weeping the twist in the story was  that in seven minutes I was weeping as  well and we were basically just handing  the box of Kleenexes back and forth  several hours later I was on the train  back to New York I looked down in my  notebook and I saw I had written about  two sentences and I realized I'm never  gonna be able to make a story out of the  it had been a very emotional experience  but nonetheless a very valuable one  because I think we did become very close  in those first meetings and I think two  seasons immense credit she eventually  shared virtually everything about her  life and her amazing relationship with  Rick the book opens with the scene where  Susan having been twice divorced was  walking her dog and a very mysterious  stranger passed by he was running in his  bare feet and to this day Susan doesn't  know why she did it but she spoke out  and said why are you running in your  bare feet and it turned out it was Rick  and he was writing a play about his  experience in Africa where he had  observed the natives in Africa off and  running in their bare feet and he wanted  to see what it felt like and naturally  that was a pretty intriguing story and  it launched a really remarkably intense  wonderful late-in-life romance between  Rick and Susan some of you you know may  be roughly my age may have read the  famous Newsweek story which said that  single women over 40 the odds of meeting  a man are lower than being struck by  lightning let me tell you that Rick and  Susan prove that that isn't that isn't  true late in the reporting I ended up  turning this into a story for The New  Yorker that was really focused on the  relationship between Rick and Susan but  rather late am i reporting for this  story Susan also mentioned that Rick had  had this amazingly close best friend  named Dan Hill and I spoke to Dan on the  phone to find out some more information  about Rick and he made an impression on  me even on the phone he lived in st.  Augustine and I remember saying several  times to Dan I said Dan you've really  had a pretty remarkable life it was  quite modest and he didn't really seem  to agree with that and but he appeared  as a character in the story although not  as a major character in the story after  the story came out he was coming up to  New York to see Susan and we were still  talking so we agreed to have dinner in  Manhattan  and I thought probably I had told this  story as much as it needed to be told  but I met Dan for dinner and Dan told me  a few more stories and I don't think  I've ever told him this but at the end  of that meal I'd hadn't even brought a  notebook I didn't write anything down  but I the first thing the next morning I  called my editor at Simon &amp; Schuster and  I said this has got to be a book you  have got to let me turn this into a book  and very shortly after that I arranged  to come spend a weekend with Dan his  wife Patricia who is also here here  today to hear more of these amazing  stories of the life and Rickon Dan that  had preceded citizens having that met  her husband including many stories I  don't think Susan herself even knew so I  landed here in in Jacksonville dan met  me at the airport and he had insisted  that I spend the weekend with with him  and his wife at their home  I haven't actually told dan this but  from past experience as a reporter I've  learned that it's sometimes it's very  risky to agree to spend the weekend with  someone's home you never quite know what  you're getting into I had a good feeling  based on my early meetings with Dan but  you never know so we pulled up to his  house and we went inside and one of the  first things I noticed is he was showing  me around the house was his quite  extensive gun collection which I'll  admit made me slightly nervous but I  think as a reporter you you learn to  trust certain instincts and within 5 or  10 minutes I I felt like part of the  family  really and ended up having a wonderful  weekend with Dan one of the stories that  he told me which was completely  unexpected was how he had met Ric when  he was working an amazing capacity and  as an undercover operative in the Congo  in 1960 and Ric was fighting in Rhodesia  with the British police force there they  met after a rugby game became instant  close friends one of these almost  magical encounters in life that Ric  seemed to experience and Dan moved in  with Ric for a while  in the barracks in what was then  northern rhodesia one day one of the  natives came in to say that a lion was  taraf terrorizing a nearby village and  could Rick do anything to help so Rick  went to the hardware store whatever it  was the general store I got this  double-barreled shotgun hopped into the  back of the Land Rover with a guy and  off they'd want to find this blond and  you can read the whole story and all its  glory in the book but to make a long  story short the lion was there that some  down the line appeared Rick had this  plan where he was going to be in this  blind and they would fire as the as the  law and attacked a a goat that they had  tied up in the middle of a clearing  but his adrenaline was rushing and for  some reason before the lion had a chance  to attack the goat Rick just leaped out  into the clearing exposing himself to  the wine the wine shifted its attention  and made a massive spring towards Rick  he had two shots in the in the barrel  double-barreled gun and he fired and the  the lion was killed and fell at his feet  so he was a hero when he came back to  the village but there was a custom in  Africa at the time that the the claws  and the teeth of a lion protected you  that they had mystical powers would  protect you from life so Rick extracted  the teeth and the claws he distributed  them to thee they were blessed by the  witch doctor he distribute them to the  people in the village but he kept a two  of the teeth for himself and a shell  casing from that and today for the first  time Dan actually brought to me the  necklace with the shell casing and the  tooth of the law and now it has added  significance because Rick wore this this  tooth and the shell casing later in life  during his fighting in Vietnam and if  you don't believe that this tooth has a  mystical power read the account of his  exploits in Vietnam and  he somehow survived wearing this too  many years later after Rick and dad had  fought together in Vietnam brick had  moved to the United States become an  American citizen dan who converted to  Islam went to fight in Afghanistan  against the Soviet occupying forces and  to protect him Rick gave Dan the tooth  and the shell casing which to this day  dad wears and which is why we've seen it  for the first time today but of course I  couldn't help but notice that that meant  on September 11th Rick no longer was  wearing the lion's tooth I'm not the  kind of person who typically believes in  this kind of thing but those are the  facts and I leave it to you to draw your  own conclusion while I was here in  Florida Dan also he pulled out a  notebook and it was stained and water  stained and at handwriting  rather neat handwriting fortunately and  it was a diary of his time in Vietnam  many years before and he said you know I  haven't looked at this in years but you  might find this interesting  I didn't read it till I got back to New  York and as I started looking through it  again I had this incredible sense of an  opportunity gift that had presented to  me because it was a very vivid and  detailed immediate account of things  that had happened many years before and  from a writer's perspective I have to  say there is like no greater gift than  that kind of raw material you don't have  to rely so much on people's fading  memories over the years it was so vivid  it was so well written it really brought  if when you see the book I think you'll  see it brought those chapters and those  experiences with Rick to vivid life I  had another experience with a diary  because later in fact after the New York  historico mode I was again talking to  Susan for the book and she had mentioned  to me that in the weeks before September  11th Rick was having trouble sleeping  and so she suggested that that he got up  in the night and write down his thaw  it's that perhaps that would she had  read somewhere that that would help  clear his head and enable him to get  back to sleep so he did and she had  found the notebook after he died in  which waking up at night he had gone and  written down what was preoccupying him  and she had been very hesitant to turn  this over to me but one morning she  handed me the notebook and she said this  is the notebook and I immediately opened  it up and started reading and it was an  incredibly powerful experience I mean  these were Rick's own words in his  handwriting it was as though he was  speaking directly to me despite his  death on September 11th and amazingly  although they were written before  September 11th he couldn't actually have  known what was about to happen they were  filled with the most profound thoughts  about the nature of heroism the nature  of courage the obligations that one has  to one's one's fellow man and at the end  of the notebook he had been thinking of  writing an autobiography himself about  his remarkable life and he had written  some titles down that he was thinking of  using for the book and the first title  on the list was heart of a soldier and  the minute I saw that it was like a  chill went down my spine again I grabbed  the phone I called my editor in New York  and I said this has got to be the title  it was almost as though Rick himself he  did in fact name the book that would be  about him before he died now in this  material in the vivid recollections of  Susan the vivid recollections of down  the raw materials of the notebooks as  well as many others I interviewed every  surviving member of Rick's platoon in  Vietnam I interviewed people who had  worked with him he had an amazing life  in various turning points in world  history  I interviewed so many that people  remembered him who also knew Dan who  nooses and I realized at some point that  I finally I felt did have the raw  material to write a story that if not  entirely living up to it to Wolf's  charge would at least attempt to grapple  with some of those big themes  I had read last year in another book by  Jacques Barzun a book called  from dawn to decadence it's a social and  cultural history of the West from Middle  Ages to the president he had said that  the notion of the epoch no longer exists  in our culture because we live in such a  fragmented society that no one can agree  on any one cause it would be worth  giving one's life for the notion of the  epic the hero in classic literature was  someone who chooses duty over  self-interest and that Duty reflected  something some principle some value the  transcended divisions and in fact  unified people for the greater good I  was thinking about this just last week  when I went to a production in New York  of the Trojans which tells the very  familiar Greek story of the Trojan horse  coming into Troy Cassandra the the  character who warns of impending  disaster but no one will listen and then  the hero Aeneas who goes to Carthage and  falls in love with with Dido  but because the gods speak to him that  he has a greater distant he leaves the  woman he loves behind and sails to Italy  where he founds what becomes the Roman  Empire and I saw so many parallels not  obvious ones but clear ones to the story  of Rick dan and Susan one of the  fascinating parts of the book which I  won't repeat because you can read it but  is the way that van and Rick  investigated the security at the World  Trade Center before 1993 and saw the  weaknesses Dan when and consulted with  Rick again after the bombing in 1993 and  in a written report they warned of the  possibility of a plane crashing in to  those towers these were the warnings of  impending doom that no one would listen  to in New York and believe me I believe  the story of how the Port Authority  responded to security issues at the  World Trade Center still remains to be  told many questions have not been  answered but just like Cassandra these  were the warnings that went unheeded and  then at that pivotal moment with chaos  all around him and tried  looming Rick had to make that very  personal and in my view heroic decision  to leave behind the people he cared most  about the woman he loved and his best  friend just as Aeneas had to leave  behind Dido  and he went back into the tower in  pursuit of a cause I think we could  agree despite what bars on said was a  cause worth dying for  he saved 3,700 lives that day even  though he gave his own and I'm not one  also to necessarily believe in in  destiny and yet in seeing the facts of  this story and I hope you will ponder  this yourself I often was amazed at the  twists of fade the way that Rick somehow  escaped death in amazing circumstances  so that he was alive at that moment in  history were his particular brand of  courage and principled integrity was so  much needed I don't like to say what  this book means exactly because I think  like a novel that's something that that  readers need to to see for themselves  but it certainly caused me to reflect on  made many big issues I grew up in the in  the era of Vietnam War protests I don't  think I really ever understood the  Vietnam War from the point of view of  people who fought there until I got to  know Rick and Dan and I think I learned  a tremendous amount about the values of  the military the best values of the  military and some of the dangerous  values of the military  neither Rick nor Dan were simple minded  soldiers simply carrying out orders in  fact one of the amazing things about the  story I think is that in saving people  in the World Trade Center Ric defied the  orders of the Port Authority he'd keep  to keep people in those towers the Port  Authority was on the loudspeaker saying  stay at your desk and Rick countermanded  those orders and got all of his people  into the stairwell that is why so many  people escaped from Morgan Stanley but  he understood the qualities of military  leadership that at their best had been  so effectively cultivated  in the American military and I think  there are embedded in the story very  deep religious and spiritual overtones  that the events of the story can cannot  be explained simply by recourse to a  rational  entirely rational universe and this is  these values are very ecumenical and  non-sectarian in fact I don't think you  could find a cast of characters that  span the the world's major religions in  this way Dan himself converted from  Catholicism to Islam Rick had been  raised as an Anglican in England but had  come to embrace many Buddhist tenets and  philosophies and susan is a church-going  Episcopalian so are there many religious  threads that weave through the story and  yet I think you find that they're rude  the same common principles that in fact  unify the world's great religions not  divide them I like to say that this is  to me not in the end really a book about  September 11th it's something that  transcends September 11th and at least  for me in the face of that tragedy has  given me tremendous hope and inspiration  there was a scene in the story that I  found particularly moving one that Dan  described to me quite late in in the  interviewing process but just in April  of the year he died Rick was inducted  into the Officer Candidate Hall of Fame  at Fort Benning Georgia and Dan went to  see Rick received this great honor there  and it turned out was the last time they  were ever together but after a day of  you know ceremonies and camaraderie and  I have to say quite a bit of drinking  it's a  there's a fair amount of alcohol in this  story but a Rick turned to Dan and and  in his very typical way he always asked  questions he never gave orders he simply  asked questions and he turned to Dan and  said are we all that we could have  become this was late in their careers  dan had had a heart attack Rick was  suffering from prostate cancer at a time  when I'm sure most people would have  been entirely justified in resting and  retiring and simply settling down to  enjoy their remaining days  Rick was still trying to become more  than he had become he was questioning  whether they had achieved all that a  human being could achieve in their  remarkable laws and all I can say is  that on September 11th he did become all  that he could and that's something I try  to think about every day am i all that I  could have become can I do more with the  qualities that have been to study state  upon me and that to me in the end is the  message I take from Rick and one that I  hope will inspire many other readers as  well thank you  James Stewart is author Oh den of  thieves bloodsport and his latest heart  of a soldier it's published by Simon &amp;  Schuster on the web at Simon Says  dot-com
 

